% int_ne(x, x): the degenerate disequality, whose term list is empty once normalised.
%
% Compile.normalise_terms merges the two occurrences of x into a coefficient of 0 and
% drops it, so what reaches the propagator is the empty sum <> 0 -- which is false, and
% is the *ground contradiction* case Compile's header argues must be posted rather than
% special-cased. It is worth a model because the two halves have to agree about it with
% no variable left to disagree over: Encoding.add_int_lin_ne's A/B pair degenerates to
% two contradictory units on its own auxiliary Boolean, and Ne.propagate reports a
% conflict whose clause is empty. The proof is `rup >= 1 ;` against those two units.
%
% A model whose only content is a contradiction over no variables at all was checked
% against veripb when M1-T14 went in (Compile's ground-constraint comment); this is the
% disequality's version of it, and the one that has an auxiliary variable in play.
var 1..3: x :: output_var;
constraint int_ne(x, x);
solve satisfy;
